hume-dialogues-2.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1 has nothing from it; hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1.2 and hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1.3 have "resembles_animal" and "like_principle"; they differ when only "the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body" is true, which the first allows
hume-dialogues-2.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1.2 and hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body" is true and "the universe seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion" is false. Is S1, "Now, if we survey the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, it bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body, and seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion.", true then?
hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1 and hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1.2 has "resembles_animal ∧ like_principle ∧ circulation_no_disorder ∧ waste_repaired ∧ sympathy ∧ parts_preserve → world_animal ∧ deity_soul"; they differ when only "the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body", "the universe seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion", "a continual circulation of matter in the universe produces no disorder", "a continual waste in every part of the universe is incessantly repaired", "the closest sympathy is perceived throughout the entire system", "each part or member, in performing its proper offices, operates both to its own preservation and to that of the whole" and "the world is an animal" are true, which the first allows
hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1.2 takes for granted "if (the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body and the universe seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion and a continual circulation of matter in the universe produces no disorder and a continual waste in every part of the universe is incessantly repaired and the closest sympathy is perceived throughout the entire system and each part or member, in performing its proper offices, operates both to its own preservation and to that of the whole), then (the world is an animal and the Deity is the soul of the world, actuating it and actuated by it)"; hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1 and hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1.3 do not
hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps hume-dialogues-2.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body and the universe seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion and a continual circulation of matter in the universe produces no disorder and a continual waste in every part of the universe is incessantly repaired and the closest sympathy is perceived throughout the entire system and each part or member, in performing its proper offices, operates both to its own preservation and to that of the whole), then (the world is an animal and the Deity is the soul of the world, actuating it and actuated by it)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
